{"name":"Sea Nymph","hex":"#8aaea4","fetched":"2026-10-13","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"sea-nymph","whole":true,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A nymph residing in the sea; a Nereid."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/sea-nymph"}],"entries":[{"title":"Amphibolis","blocks":[{"text":"Amphibolis is a genus in the family Cymodoceaceae. It includes two species of sea grass endemic to the western and southern coast of Australia, Amphibolis antarctica and Amphibolis griffithii, commonly known as sea nymph or wire weed."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/b7/Amphibolis_antarctica_1859.jpg/330px-Amphibolis_antarctica_1859.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Amphibolis"},{"title":"Nereids","blocks":[{"text":"In Greek mythology, the Nereids or Nereides are sea nymphs, the 50 daughters of the 'Old Man of the Sea' Nereus and the Oceanid Doris, sisters to their brother Nerites. They often accompany Poseidon, the god of the sea, and can be friendly and helpful to sailors."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/3d/Neptune%27s_Triumph_mosaic_from_the_House_of_Sorothus_in_ancient_Hadrumetus_%28Sousse%29%2C_dated_to_the_2nd_century_AD%2C_National_Museum_of_Bardo%2C_Tunisia_%2854178190251%29.jpg/330px-thumbnail.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Nereids"},{"title":"Sea","blocks":[{"text":"A sea is a very large body of salty water. The term has two distinct meanings. The first is the interconnected body of seawater that spans most of Earth, known as the sea or the ocean. The second is a named body of water: usually either a marginal sea, or a large, nearly landlocked body of water."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/1c/Mediterranean_Sea_16.61811E_38.99124N.jpg/330px-Mediterranean_Sea_16.61811E_38.99124N.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sea"},{"title":"Nymph","blocks":[{"text":"A nymph is a minor female nature deity in ancient Greek folklore. Distinct from other Greek goddesses, nymphs are generally regarded as personifications of nature; they are typically tied to a specific place, landform, or tree, and are usually depicted as maidens. Because of their association with springs, they were often seen as having healing properties; other divine powers of the nymphs included divination and shapeshifting. In spite of their divine nature, they were not immortal."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/31/Three_nymphs%2C_marble_relief%2C_c._320%E2%80%93300_BC.jpg/330px-Three_nymphs%2C_marble_relief%2C_c._320%E2%80%93300_BC.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Nymph"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/7b/French_Empire_mantel_clock.jpg/500px-French_Empire_mantel_clock.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/7b/French_Empire_mantel_clock.jpg/1280px-French_Empire_mantel_clock.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/7b/French_Empire_mantel_clock.jpg/500px-French_Empire_mantel_clock.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"French Empire mantel clock (1822) depicting the nereid Galatea velificans","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AFrench_Empire_mantel_clock.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/69/Sea_thiasos_Nereis_Glyptothek_Munich_239_front_n1.jpg/500px-Sea_thiasos_Nereis_Glyptothek_Munich_239_front_n1.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/comm